                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "233759175",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561199817855802",
                            "num_reviews": 2478,
                            "playtime_forever": 124,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 124,
                            "last_played": 1787693487
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Dragon's Lunch is a really unusual roguelike because it takes the traditional dungeon crawling formula and makes the whole thing happen in real time. I start off playing golf, somehow end up falling down an absurdly deep rabbit hole and wake up in a suit of armour inside a dungeon. My goal is simple enough. I need to find my lost golf ball while making my way through twelve dangerous levels and eventually dealing with the dragon waiting at the bottom. The whole golf setup is ridiculous, but I actually like how much personality it gives what could otherwise be a fairly standard dungeon game.\n\nThe real time system is the thing that makes it stand out for me. Traditional roguelikes usually give me plenty of time to think about every move, but here the monsters keep moving while I am looking around, checking my map or deciding what to do. There is no normal pause, so I have to stay aware of what is happening around me. I can still use the familiar roguelike ideas of exploring, finding potions, reading scrolls, using weapons and armour, firing wands and collecting rings, but I have to make those decisions much more quickly. I like the extra pressure because even checking something can become dangerous when an enemy is nearby.\n\nThe randomly generated dungeons give me a good reason to replay it. Each new game can create a different layout, so I cannot simply memorise one perfect route through the dungeon. There is also a built in level editor, which is something I did not expect from such a small game. I can make my own maps or play the included custom content, including the twelve level Underpar adventure. That gives the game more value than just completing the main randomly generated runs once. I also like that I can use the same random seed if I want to recreate a particular dungeon.\n\nThe permanent death system is where the game gets properly tense. If I die, that run is finished, and I have to start again. There are different modes that change how saving works, with Putter mode allowing saves, Wedge mode only allowing limited reloading and Iron mode removing saving completely. I like having those options because I can decide how seriously I want to take the roguelike rules. The harder modes definitely make every mistake feel more important, while the easier saving option gives me a way to enjoy the exploration without risking losing a long run quite as easily.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that the real time system can also make things feel awkward. I like the idea, but there are moments where I want to stop and properly think about an item, map or situation, and the game does not really give me that luxury. The presentation is also very basic, which is understandable for a small indie roguelike, but it means the atmosphere depends heavily on the mechanics rather than visual detail. Some of the older design choices can feel rough as well, and I can imagine needing a little patience before I understand exactly how everything works. Still, I think the unusual real time approach gives the simple presentation a reason to exist.\n\nI also appreciate how much the developer has packed into the basic concept. There are twelve levels, traps, puzzles, different monsters, traditional roguelike equipment, multiple game modes, achievements and the map editor. The game was released in 2017, but the developer has continued working on it and released a newer version through itch.io as a pay what you like game, which shows that the project has continued to receive attention rather than simply being abandoned after release.\n\nOverall, Dragon's Lunch is a strange but genuinely interesting roguelike that I think gets most of its appeal from the decision to make everything happen in real time. I like the random dungeons, permanent death, different saving modes, traditional dungeon equipment and the ability to create my own maps. The simple presentation and occasionally awkward real time gameplay stop it from being one of my favourite roguelikes, but the concept is different enough that I remember it. I think it works best when I want something challenging and unusual rather than a polished modern dungeon crawler. The ridiculous idea of searching a dungeon for a golf ball while trying not to become the dragon's lunch gives the whole thing a charm that I did not expect.\n\nRating: 6.7/10\n",
